// A `for` reacts to what a binding along another tree leads to. What it
// changes there itself is an event for an entity it may have been at
// already: nothing says which of the two comes first. That event is not
// lost: it counts the next time the `for` runs. (What the `for` would
// read there it may not change, so no entity sees an old value: a `for`
// that is to see what it does to others in the same frame is two, in a
// `loop` until neither has anything to do.)
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/late_events.ent 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/late_events.ent 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s

// CHECK:      1: 3 ran
// The third, whose `h` another system changed, and the second, beside it.
// CHECK:      2: 2 ran
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 1: h 1 runs 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 2: h 2 runs 2
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: 3: h 30 runs 2
// The third again, and the second for what the `for` did to the third.
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: 2 ran
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: 1: h 1 runs 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: 2: h 2 runs 3
// CHECK-NEXT: 3: 3: h 30 runs 3
// CHECK-NEXT: 4: 0 ran
